Rules the learning element extracted at the final learn(), per arm
  Arm A:
    - SAMPLE SIZE CAVEAT: These 6 episodes contain zero repeated (type, urgency, tier) states, so every rule below rests on n=1 to n=3 observations. Treat them as hypotheses to test, not policies to deploy.
    - STRONGEST SIGNAL — 'timeout' appears in 3/3 failures and 0/3 successes, and appears even in the one case where success=true but the customer was dissatisfied (complaint/high/free, reward -0.5). This suggests timeout is the dominant failure driver, possibly a system/infrastructure issue rather than a consequence of action choice. Investigate timeout root cause before changing action-selection rules.
    - DO NOT conclude 'avoid request_info' — request_info produced the single best-reward success (account_change/medium/standard, +3.0) AND both hard failures. The action alone does not separate outcomes; something else (likely timeout) does. Confounded evidence.
    - DO NOT conclude 'avoid auto_resolve on complaints' — auto_resolve gave +3.0 (complaint/low/premium) and -0.5 (complaint/high/free). The two differ in BOTH urgency and tier simultaneously, so neither variable can be isolated. Needs a controlled comparison (e.g., complaint/high/premium and complaint/low/free) before acting.
    - WEAK BUT TESTABLE — Reward appears inversely related to handling time among successes: 10s→3.0, 22s→3.0, 47s→2.5. Consistent with a latency penalty in the reward function. Verify against the reward spec directly rather than inferring from 3 points.
    - WEAK BUT TESTABLE — account_change is the only type with failures (2 of 3 attempts), all via request_info, all timing out at 28-33s versus the 22s success. Hypothesis: request_info on account_change sits near a timeout threshold. Test by instrumenting the request_info latency distribution.
    - NO EVIDENCE ON TIER — premium appears in 2 successes and 1 failure, free in 2 failures, standard in 1 success. Tier is too sparsely and unevenly sampled to support any tier-based routing rule; do not build tier priority logic on this data.
    - NO EVIDENCE ON write_custom — observed exactly once (billing_question/low/premium, +2.5). Cannot be compared to alternatives on the same state. Its 47s duration is the slowest observed and worth watching, but one sample proves nothing.
    - NEXT STEP TO DE-CONFOUND: collect repeated trials of the same state with different actions (especially complaint/high across tiers, and account_change with request_info vs auto_resolve) and log whether timeouts correlate with action, state, or wall-clock/load conditions.
  Arm B:
    - PATTERN (strong but confounded): Tier perfectly separates outcomes in this sample — all 3 successes were standard/premium, all 3 failures were free tier. Because tier and action never vary independently here, tier and action effects cannot be disentangled from these 6 cases alone.
    - DO MORE: Use write_custom for premium-tier tickets. It succeeded 2/2 (complaint/medium/premium, outage/high/premium) with the fastest success times (59s, 66s) and satisfied customers.
    - DO MORE: Escalate high-urgency outages when a fast path is unavailable — escalate succeeded on outage/high/standard (105s). Note this is the slowest success, so prefer write_custom if the agent can handle it directly.
    - AVOID: send_template as the primary response — 0/2 success (account_change/low/free, complaint/medium/free). Templates appear to under-serve tickets that need substantive resolution.
    - AVOID: request_info as a first move on account_change — 1/1 failure with a timeout, suggesting the customer disengaged rather than supplying details. Prefer resolving with available data or escalating.
    - SIGNAL: Very short handling times (11-28s) accompanied all 3 failures, while all successes took 59-105s. Treat sub-30s resolution as a warning sign of a low-effort action, not as efficiency.
    - SIGNAL: 'timeout' errors appeared only in the two actions that push work back to the customer (request_info, send_template) on free-tier account_change tickets. Actions requiring customer follow-up carry timeout risk; minimize round-trips.
    - COMPLAINT TICKETS: write_custom succeeded (premium) where send_template failed (free). For complaints, invest in a custom response rather than a template.
    - UNTESTED GAP — do not over-generalize: write_custom and escalate were never tried on free tier, and send_template/request_info were never tried on standard/premium. The rule 'free tier always fails' is unproven; test write_custom on free-tier tickets before concluding tier drives the outcome.
    - EXPERIMENT NEXT: (a) write_custom on free-tier account_change and complaint; (b) send_template on a premium ticket. These two probes would reveal whether the driver is tier, action, or ticket type.
    - LOW CONFIDENCE OVERALL: 6 episodes, 3 per class, no repeated state-action pair. Treat all rules above as tentative priors and keep exploring rather than locking in a policy.
    - ACCOUNT_CHANGE is the weakest category (0/2, both timeouts) and has no successful example yet — flag these tickets for a different strategy and gather data before committing to a default action.

